## Maintenance changes and ownership

- `native.go`: constructs arena offsets and calls `rn_verify_v2`. Checks the
  success bitmap against every per-item status, including bit 63. A disagreement
  is an execution failure. Wire results and error normalization are unchanged.
- `wire.go`: accepts absent/normal/high priority, defaulting to normal. Priority
  remains separate from the payload digest. Invalid priority has no state effect.
- `state.go`: persists priority and accepts both priorities during recovery.
  Missing legacy priority means normal; explicit empty, null, non-string, or
  unknown priority is rejected. Recovery does not rewrite pending records or
  receipts merely to upgrade them. Incompatible schemas still fail.
- `scheduler.go`, `server.go`: coordinator-owned FIFO high and normal queues.
  When both contain work, dispatch follows high/high/high/normal. With just one
  nonempty queue, dispatch without advancing or resetting the cycle. Empty queues
  also preserve the cycle. Restart begins a new high-first cycle. Recovery inserts
  pending work in admission order within each priority.

Four workers still share native execution slots with nonpersistent evaluate
requests. Evaluate accepts the same optional priority and joins that queue;
absent priority is normal. Worker completion may differ from dispatch order.
Only the next admission sequence can commit terminally. Identical retries keep
the original sequence and priority; changed payload digests return conflict.

Worker-owned native backing and arena survive until native return, including
cancellation. Admission, cancellation intent, and one-job terminal transitions
retain synchronous WAL-enabled WriteBatches. Finished work retains count/byte
charges until durable terminal commit. The coordinator owns cancellation through
terminal C write entry and return. Native failure on acknowledged work stops
with its pending record intact; evaluate failure returns execution_failure.
Storage failures are not acknowledged. Existing transport ordering, bounds,
disconnect behavior, receipt retention, writer lock, and drain are preserved.

